\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Multi-turn jailbreak attacks pose a qualitatively different threat to large language model (LLM) safety than their single-turn counterparts. Attacks such as Crescendo~\citep{russinovich2024crescendo}, Foot-in-the-Door (FITD)~\citep{fitd2024}, and Deceptive Delight~\citep{deceptivedelight2024} distribute adversarial intent across individually benign turns through rapport building, logical reframing, and incremental escalation. Persuasion is the common mechanism: \citet{zeng2024pap} catalogue 40 persuasion techniques across jailbreak families, and \citet{hagendorff2026reasoning} find identifiable strategies in 97\% of successful attacks. Individual attack turns occupy the same embedding region as benign text~\citep{bullwinkel2025repeng}, rendering per-turn safety filters fundamentally blind.

Production classifiers such as Llama Guard~\citep{inan2023llamaguard}, WildGuard~\citep{han2024wildguard}, and ShieldGemma~\citep{zeng2024shieldgemma} operate on individual turns and cannot model manipulation trajectories across exchanges. Recent multi-turn defenses have begun to address conversation-level structure: DeepContext~\citep{deepcontext2026} aggregates BERT embeddings with a recurrent network, BIID~\citep{tong2025biid} infers turn-level intentions, and G-Guard~\citep{gguard2025} applies graph neural networks. These systems achieve reasonable in-distribution performance but degrade on unseen attack types, and none systematically analyze \emph{why} some representations generalize while others fail. The open question is: \emph{what representation properties enable cross-attack generalization?}

A vanilla DeBERTa encoder~\citep{he2021deberta} with temporal aggregation collapses from F1\,=\,1.000 on in-distribution data to F1\,=\,0.284 on out-of-distribution (OOD) Deceptive Delight, worsening with more turns (0.575 at turn~1 to 0.284 at full conversation). LLM-as-judge approaches fare no better: GPT-5.5 achieves F1 of only 0.191--0.449 on in-distribution detection with 30--47\% unparseable responses (Appendix~\ref{sec:llm_judge}). Content-level reasoning is insufficient for this task.

We built \textbf{DisPeD} (Discourse-level Persuasion Detection), a two-stage framework with seven controlled Stage~1 variants that systematically decompose the mechanism: 9-class persuasion strategy classification, binary classification, scrambled labels, MLM only, topic classification, Wikipedia MLM, and vanilla control (Section~\ref{sec:method}). Each variant isolates a specific factor (domain exposure, classification supervision, label quality, label granularity, or domain specificity) while holding all other components constant, enabling causal attribution of detection performance to individual factors.

The result \emph{partially falsified} our original hypothesis that persuasion-specific classification is the key ingredient. MLM without labels achieves near-perfect early detection on unseen Deceptive Delight (mean F1\,$\geq$\,0.97 at $K \leq 5$), but shows instability at full conversation length (F1\,=\,0.91$\pm$0.12) while the 9-class model maintains 0.997$\pm$0.006. The evidence hierarchy is:

\vspace{2pt}
\noindent\hspace{0.5em}{\small vanilla (0.28) $<$ topic (0.54$\pm$0.21) $\ll$ wiki-MLM (0.90$\pm$0.12) $\approx$ jb-MLM (0.91$\pm$0.12) $<$ scrambled (0.99) $\approx$ binary (0.99) $\approx$ 9-class (0.997$\pm$0.006)}
\vspace{2pt}

\noindent Any continued pretraining bridges the dominant $\sim$62\,pp gap from vanilla. Wikipedia MLM nearly matches jailbreak-domain MLM at full length; the domain advantage is meaningful at early prefixes ($K$=2: 0.993 vs.\ 0.911) and for perturbation robustness. Only the 9-class model achieves F1\,=\,0.997$\pm$0.006 with near-zero variance. This connects to domain-adaptive pretraining~\citep{gururangan2020dontstop}: continued training captures pragmatic features, with classification supervision providing stability that unsupervised pretraining alone does not guarantee.

Our contributions:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*, itemsep=2pt, topsep=4pt]
  \item \textbf{Systematic decomposition of cross-attack generalization.} Seven controlled variants establish that continued pretraining bridges $\sim$62\,pp of the OOD gap; jailbreak-domain text improves early detection ($K$=2: 8\,pp over Wikipedia MLM). Misaligned supervision (topic F1\,=\,0.54$\pm$0.21) destabilizes generalization.

  \item \textbf{Classification supervision adds stability, robustness, and interpretability.} Classification-supervised variants achieve F1\,$\geq$\,0.99 with near-zero variance vs.\ MLM 0.91$\pm$0.12. Under perturbation ($n$=76), all classification-supervised variants (including scrambled labels) show zero degradation at full length vs.\ $\sim$12\,pp for non-adapted models. A three-layer robustness attribution emerges: classification structure provides full-length robustness, label granularity adds $K$=2 robustness, and correct fine-grained labels yield the strongest early-turn robustness (9-class $\Delta$=$-$2.6\,pp vs.\ scrambled $-$5.3\,pp vs.\ binary $-$9.2\,pp at $K$=1). The persuasion taxonomy additionally provides distinct strategy trajectories per attack type (Section~\ref{sec:strategy}).
\end{itemize}

